+# Landmark sources: set pieces 13–17
+
+Checked on 8 November 2026 against primary official sources. These are the Taichung Tourism and Travel Bureau, the Taiwan Tourism Administration, the National Museum of Natural Science, the Taichung City Cultural Heritage Office, the Taichung City Government and its bureaus and district offices, and the temple's own management office. All descriptions are short paraphrases. Hours and prices appear only where they show whether a place is open.
+
+Reading notes:
+- Coordinates come from each tourism entry's `place:location:latitude`/`longitude` HTML metadata, cross-checked against the map link on the page. They are representative points, not door positions. Latitude is given first.
+- The tourism bureau's `Dc.Description` metadata on every page holds the bureau's own office address in Fengyuan, not the landmark's address. Ignore it.
+- The zh-tw tourism pages build their body text with JavaScript, so they were read in a browser. The en pages serve their text as static HTML.
+- Distances and compass directions below are straight-line values computed from the official points, rounded. Neighbour points: Taichung Park `24.14431, 120.68571`, Old Taichung Station `24.13678, 120.68501`, Miyahara `24.13784, 120.68356` (all from `docs/landmark-sources.md`), and the National Museum of Natural Science `24.157233, 120.66606` (Taiwan Tourism Administration). The Green River uses the tourism bureau's Lyuchuan Canal point, `24.13900, 120.68451`. Its official English name is [Lyuchuan Canal / 綠川](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/intro/1597), and that point sits on the stretch by the station and Miyahara. The river is a long channel, and its full course was not checked against an official map.
+
+## 13. Botanical Garden / 科博館植物園 (Tropical Rainforest Greenhouse / 熱帶雨林溫室)
+
+Verdict: verified. The plan note is right about the place. It is wrong about the shape.
+
+- [NMNS: Botanical Garden (en)](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/en/exhibitions/galleries/botanical-garden/index.html) and [植物園 (zh)](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/ch/exhibitions/galleries/botanical-garden/index.html): the 4.5-hectare garden of Taiwanese lowland ecology zones, with a tall rainforest greenhouse. The zh page calls the greenhouse a pagoda-like glass greenhouse with a Chinese flavour (倣塔造型), about 40 m tall overall. Garden and greenhouse together hold more than 800 plant species.
+- [NMNS: Tropical Rainforest Greenhouse (en)](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/en/exhibitions/galleries/botanical-garden/tropical-rain-forest-greenhouse/index.html): a giant glass greenhouse built on six sets of columns and seven circular beams, called the garden's most prominent landmark. It has themed rainforest sections and an Amazon fish aquarium in the basement.
+- [NMNS: 熱帶雨林溫室 (zh)](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/ch/exhibitions/galleries/botanical-garden/tropical-rain-forest-greenhouse/index.html): a suspended steel frame carrying a glass curtain wall. It stands 31 m above ground, goes 4.5 m below ground and is 56 m across. It has about 2,500 m² of display and about 500 species, plus an 8th-floor viewing deck. Simulated downpours and waterfalls run along the paths.
+- [NMNS: Museum history](https://www.nmns.edu.tw/en/about/museum-history/): the city gave 44,856 m² of land, and the Botanical Garden opened to the public on 23 July 1999. The page gives no separate date for the greenhouse.
+- [Taichung Tourism: National Museum of Natural Science - Botanical Garden (en)](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/intro/902) and [國立自然科學博物館之植物園 (zh)](https://taichung.travel/zh-tw/attractions/intro/158): these repeat the museum's description. The zh entry also gives 4.9 ha in one paragraph and about 400 species in the greenhouse. The museum's own figures (4.5 ha, about 500 species) are preferred.
+- Names: the museum uses Botanical Garden / 植物園 and Tropical Rainforest Greenhouse / 熱帶雨林溫室. The tourism bureau titles it 國立自然科學博物館之植物園. 科博館 is the museum's official short name (tourism entry 418).
+- Address: No. 1, Guanqian Road, North District (the museum's address).
+- Coordinates: latitude `24.15812`, longitude `120.66817`, from the tourism metadata. The en entry's map link gives the same point at higher precision.
+- Look, from the museum's text and the official tourism photos: a round glass greenhouse that tapers as it rises, wrapped in an exposed light-blue steel lattice. Tall masts with pointed tips stand above the roof, ring beams run around it inside, a butterfly-wing canopy covers the entrance, and a pond lies in front.
+- Plan note check:
+  - "Rainforest glasshouse beside the Science Museum": supported.
+  - "Tall curved glass vault?": wrong. It is not a vault or a barrel shape. It is a round, tower-like glass greenhouse 56 m across and 31 m tall above ground. The museum calls it pagoda-like and about 40 m tall overall. Draw a tapered round glass drum with a steel frame and mast spikes.
+  - Age: the garden opened in July 1999. The museum does not give a separate opening date for the greenhouse.
+- Position: about 0.25 km east-northeast of the museum's representative point, in the same campus. It is about 2.4 km northwest of Taichung Park and about 2.7–2.9 km northwest of Miyahara, the Green River point and Old Taichung Station.
+- Status: open. The museum footer lists Tuesday to Sunday, 09:00–17:00. The zh greenhouse page lists the Amazon fish and piranha feeding tours as paused from March 2027 (ROC 116). It announces no closure of the greenhouse.
+
+## 14. Taichung Confucius Temple / 臺中孔廟 (official: Taichung City Confucius Temple / 臺中市孔廟)
+
+Verdict: verified. Song style, the Lingxing gate and the double-eaved hip roof are all confirmed by the temple's own management office. One government source disagrees on the roof type (below).
+
+- [Taichung Tourism: Taichung City Confucius Temple (en)](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/intro/1371) and [臺中市孔廟 (zh)](https://taichung.travel/zh-tw/attractions/intro/41): a Song dynasty palace-style complex. Planners chose that style to set it apart from the Qing-style Martyrs' Shrine next door. The plan is a scaled-down Qufu temple, and all painted decoration is in Song style.
+- The [management office (臺中市孔廟忠烈祠聯合管理所)](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/) runs its own site. Pages used:
+  - [Architecture overview](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/837602/post): the site was an air-force children's school until 1967. It was designed and supervised by the provincial public works bureau and is built in reinforced concrete. Site area is 23,653 m². Elements: two pailou, a screen wall, the Lingxing gate, the Pan pond forecourt, the Guande and Yucui gates, the Dacheng gate, the Dacheng hall, the east and west cloisters, and the Chongsheng shrine.
+  - [欞星門](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/837653/post): the first gate, 11.95 m high and 6.6 m wide. It follows the Song 烏頭門 type, an open post-and-lintel gateway. Its columns are ceremonial pillars, its doors are lattice/fence leaves, and a pair of lions sits on the column tops. Smaller 金聲 and 玉振 gates stand on each side, joined to it by short walls. The base is grey chiselled cement and the columns are white chiselled cement. It is normally kept shut, and visitors enter by the Guande gate on Shuangshi Road ([visitor map page](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/834557/834568/834569/834585/837895/post)).
+  - [泮池](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/837655/post): a half-moon pond inside the Lingxing gate, 30 m wide, with an arched bridge (碧水橋) across the middle.
+  - [大成門](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/837661/post): a five-bay gate with a single-eaved hip-and-gable roof (歇山). Its doors are red-lacquered and studded with bronze nails.
+  - [大成殿](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/837663/post) and [其他](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/837667/post): the main hall stands in the inner court. It is 27 m wide, 19 m deep, 17.53 m tall and seven bays across. Its roof is a northern Song-imitation double-eaved hip roof (重檐廡殿) in yellow glazed tile, with no ridge beasts on the hips. The porch columns are lacquered vermilion. Eaves, brackets, beams and ceiling carry Song-style painting, mainly muted blue-green with brown beams. In front is a balustraded dance terrace with a carved central stair. The east and west cloisters (廡, 68.5 m long, roll-ridge roofs) link the Dacheng gate to the Chongsheng shrine behind the hall, closing the court on all four sides.
+  - [觀德門、毓粹門](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/837665/post) and [照壁、牌坊](https://www.confucius.taichung.gov.tw/837651/post): the side gates are hip-and-gable with 青 glazed tile. The two five-bay pailou on Lixing Road (13.4 m tall) and the screen wall outside the enclosing wall are faux-granite with 青 glazed tile. 青 here could mean green or blue-green.
+- [Taiwan Tourism Administration: 臺中孔廟](https://www.taiwan.net.tw/m1.aspx?sNo=0001016&id=10351): this is where the shorter name 臺中孔廟 comes from. It says the temple stands beside the Martyrs' Shrine at Shuangshi and Lixing Roads, with two white pailou in front and the half-moon pond and bridge behind the gate. It calls the main hall roof double-eaved hip-and-gable (歇山重簷). That contradicts the temple office's 重檐廡殿, and the operator's page is preferred.
+- Names: the tourism bureau and the temple office use 臺中市孔廟 / Taichung City Confucius Temple. The Taiwan Tourism Administration uses 臺中孔廟.
+- Address: No. 30, Section 2, Shuangshi Road, North District.
+- Coordinates: latitude `24.15432`, longitude `120.68997`, from the tourism metadata. The Taiwan Tourism Administration gives longitude/latitude `120.68995 / 24.154329`.
+- Plan note check:
+  - "Song-style hall": supported.
+  - "Behind the Lingxing gate": supported, with a refinement. The Lingxing gate is an open pillar-and-lattice gateway with lions on the pillars, not a roofed gatehouse. The order from the street is the gate, the half-moon pond and bridge, then the roofed Dacheng gate, then the hall.
+  - "In a walled court": broadly supported. The hall's court is closed in by the long cloisters, the Dacheng gate and the Chongsheng shrine. An outer wall surrounds the whole complex.
+  - "Double-eaved hip roof": supported by the temple office (重檐廡殿). The Taiwan Tourism Administration says hip-and-gable instead.
+  - Colours: yellow glazed roof, vermilion columns and doors, grey faux-granite surfaces, and muted blue-green Song painting.
+  - Age: the official pages read give no construction years. Non-official sources say 1972–1976, which was not verified.
+- Position: about 1.2 km north-northeast of Taichung Park and 0.76 km northeast of the Yizhong representative point. It is about 1.8–2.0 km north-northeast of the Green River point, Miyahara and Old Taichung Station, and about 2.45 km east of the Science Museum. The Martyrs' Shrine adjoins it.
+- Status: open, free, Tuesday to Sunday 09:00–17:00 and closed Mondays (tourism bureau and temple office).
+
+## 15. Yizhong Street / 一中街 (official: Yizhong Shopping District / 一中商圈)
+
+Verdict: verified as a student shopping and food street near Taichung First Senior High School. No arch or gateway is supported.
+
+- [Taichung Tourism: Yizhong Shopping District (en)](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/intro/1551) and [一中商圈 (zh)](https://taichung.travel/zh-tw/attractions/intro/400): the district sits near Chung Yo Department Store and runs into the Sanmin shopping area. It is near Taichung First Senior High School, the National Taichung University of Science and Technology and the National Taiwan University of Sport. It is crowded with students, with snack stalls and eateries everywhere. Street food is on Yucai Street and most clothing shops are in the small lanes.
+- [Economic Development Bureau: 一中商圈](https://www.economic.taichung.gov.tw/16103/17901/1730045/17844/47989): the district grew from the student crowds of Taichung First High, the university and many cram schools. It is nicknamed Taichung's Nanyang Street (after Taipei's cram-school street). It is an ordinary shopping street by day and a food-heavy night market by night.
+- Official tourism photos show narrow pedestrian streets lined with shopfronts and stacked signboards, food stalls, a street hung with lanterns, and the iPlaza mall corner. None shows an arch or entrance gateway.
+- Names: the street is 一中街 (Yizhong Street, the official address). The attraction is Yizhong Shopping District / 一中商圈. Older tourism titles read 一中街夜市＿一中商圈.
+- Address: Yizhong Street, North District. It has no street number.
+- Coordinates: latitude `24.14914`, longitude `120.68502`, from the tourism metadata.
+- Plan note check:
+  - "Student shopping street by Taichung First High": supported.
+  - Arch or gateway: no official source mentions one, and the official photos show none. Do not add a gate. Signboards, stalls and overhead lanterns are supported.
+- Position: about 0.54 km north of Taichung Park's representative point. The tourism bureau's nearby list gives 542 m to the park. It is about 1.1–1.4 km north of the Green River point, Miyahara and Old Taichung Station. It is about 0.76 km southwest of the Confucius Temple and about 2.1 km east-southeast of the Science Museum.
+- Status: an active shopping district. The tourism zh page says hours depend on each shop.
+
+## 16. Taichung Prefectural Hall / 臺中州廳
+
+Verdict: verified. Architect, year, mansard roof and arched windows are confirmed. The wall colour, central-pavilion and fountain parts of the plan note need correcting. It reopened on 15 October 2026.
+
+- [Taichung Tourism: Taichung Prefectural Hall (en)](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/intro/1367): designed by Moriyama Matsunosuke in the Japanese era, the same architect as the Taipei and Tainan prefectural halls. It is French mansard in style, with a two-storey L-shaped plan facing east and a tower at the middle. The ground-floor entrance has Doric columns and the upper floor has Ionic columns, with recessed balconies for shade. The page carries a "Renovating" label (posted 2026-10-30) and still calls it a government building in use.
+- [Taichung Tourism: 臺中州廳 (zh)](https://taichung.travel/zh-tw/attractions/intro/56): a national monument mixing European Baroque and neoclassical styles. The main entrance is at the street corner, with wings spreading to either side. It is now an arts and exhibition venue run by the Cultural Affairs Bureau, open Tuesday to Sunday 09:00–17:00. One photo caption names its arched windows (拱型窗戶).
+- [Cultural Heritage Office: 臺中州廳](https://www.tchac.taichung.gov.tw/building?pid=18&uid=33): Moriyama Matsunosuke, a Government-General engineer, designed it after European Baroque models. The main block was begun in 1913 (Taishō 2) and its first phase finished the next year. Four further phases set its present size by 1934. The building sits on a street corner with the main entrance at the corner and wings to both sides. It was registered as a historic building in 2002, made a city monument in 2006 and a national monument on 25 April 2019. Main restoration ran 2019–2023 and reuse and landscape works 2025–2026. The Cultural Affairs Bureau moved in on 31 August 2026, and the Taichung Prefectural Hall Cultural Park (臺中州廳文化園區) opened on 15 October 2026. Address: 99 Minquan Road, West District.
+- [Taichung City Government news, 15 June 2026: 歷久彌新百年風華](https://www.taichung.gov.tw/8868/8872/9962/3297938): Cultural Affairs Bureau text. It says the building was built in 1913 and is related to the Presidential Office Building. Its main entrance at the corner was unusual for the time, with a corner tower as the focal point, a projecting carriage porch, symmetric flanking towers and long street wings. The facade uses stucco faux-stone, classical columns, a dome, triangular and rounded pediments and medallions. The roof is a double-pitched mansard with oeil-de-boeuf windows. The walls are warm red brick with grey-white faux-stone bands and a washed-pebble base. The windows have arched tops.
+- [Taichung City Government news, 15 October 2026: 台中州廳修復重啟](https://www.taichung.gov.tw/8868/8872/9962/3392631): the hall reopened as the Taichung Prefectural Hall Cultural Park after nearly ten years and over NT$600 million of work. The main building is a hollow square (口字型) around a central lawn. About 40% of the space is for arts, and more than 4,200 ping of outdoor green space is open. Captions mention the mansard roof, bull's-eye windows and dormers.
+- [Taichung City Government news, 2023: 百年古蹟台中州廳限定開放](https://www.taichung.gov.tw/2518459/post): the mansard roof has two pitches and carries dormers (老虎窗) and bull's-eye windows. The exterior imitates classical architecture mixed with early colonial veranda style.
+- Official tourism photo (cover image of the zh/en entries): a white-stucco corner block with a columned carriage porch under a low dome, and a white columned central bay with a curved gable. The long wings are red brick with white horizontal bands and arched windows. The mansard roof is dark grey with dormers, and palm trees stand along the front.
+- Coordinates: latitude `24.13856`, longitude `120.67848`, from the tourism metadata on both language entries, matching the en map link. The Cultural Heritage Office map marker is `24.1393410, 120.6789550`.
+- Plan note check:
+  - "Japanese-era civic hall": supported. Architect Moriyama Matsunosuke, begun 1913, expanded in phases to 1934. Style is described as mansard, Baroque and neoclassical.
+  - "White walls": wrong as stated. The Cultural Affairs Bureau describes red-brick walls with grey-white bands and a pebble-wash base, and the official photo agrees. White stucco covers mainly the corner entrance block, the porch and the columns. Draw red brick with white bands, and white stucco only on the entrance block.
+  - "Arched windows": supported.
+  - "Slate mansard roof": the mansard is supported (two pitches, dormers, bull's-eye windows). No source says slate. The photo shows dark grey, so a dark grey roof is safe but "slate" is unsupported.
+  - "Central pavilion": partly. The main entrance is at the street corner. It has a corner tower, a projecting carriage porch with a low dome and paired columns, and symmetric flanking towers. The en tourism page does describe a tower in the middle of the L-shaped front. A central pavilion reads correctly only if the corner faces the viewer. Give it a columned porch and a small dome.
+  - "Forecourt fountain": unsupported. No official source mentions a fountain. The sources describe a central lawn inside the hollow-square plan and open green space around the park. Leave out the fountain, or use lawn.
+- Position: about 0.52 km west of Miyahara and 0.6 km west of the Green River point. It is about 0.7 km west-northwest of Old Taichung Station and about 1 km southwest of Taichung Park. It is about 0.42 km south of the Second Market (the tourism nearby list gives 424 m) and about 2.4 km south-southeast of the Science Museum. The Liuchuan Canal waterfront point is about 0.47 km north-northwest.
+- Status: open since 15 October 2026 as the Taichung Prefectural Hall Cultural Park (Cultural Heritage Office, city news and the zh tourism entry). The en tourism entry's "Renovating" label is out of date.
+
+## 17. Second Market / 第二市場 (formal: 臺中市公有第二零售市場)
+
+Verdict: verified for the hexagonal tower and the market's age. "Beside the Green River" is wrong. It is next to the Liuchuan Canal.
+
+- [Taichung Tourism: Second Market (en)](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/intro/1366): a market about 100 years old at Sanmin Road and Taiwan Boulevard. In the Japanese era it sold high-end goods. A historic hexagonal building marks its centre, and vendors were grouped by trade around a hexagonal layout.
+- [Taichung Tourism: 第二市場 (zh)](https://taichung.travel/zh-tw/attractions/intro/70): formal name 臺中市公有第二零售市場. Hexagonal radial aisles all meet at the central 六角樓, which earned the nickname 六條通. It has over 300 stalls. It is over a century old, was called 新富町市場 in the Japanese era and was nicknamed the Japanese market. The city has repaired it. One photo is captioned 六角樓.
+- [Central District Office: 第二市場及六角樓](https://www.central.taichung.gov.tw/1699766/post): the public market began in 1917. The main structure has three radial wings with a hexagonal main building at the core, and the hexagonal tower dates from 1936.
+- [Taichung Tourism: Second Market nearby attractions](https://travel.taichung.gov.tw/en/attractions/nearbyattractions/1366): the Liuchuan Canal Waterfront is 171 m away. Lyuchuan Canal (the Green River) does not appear in the list, which runs out to 551 m.
+- Official photo of the 六角樓 entrance: a two-storey corner building. The lower floor is red brick with grey wash-stone frames, and a grey lintel carries the name 六角樓 in gold. The upper floor is pale stucco with a shaped, stepped gable parapet and an arched window behind a balcony rail. Other photos show covered aisles with roller-shutter stalls and mural-painted entrances.
+- Address: No. 87, Section 2, Sanmin Road, Central District.
+- Coordinates: latitude `24.14236`, longitude `120.67882`, from the tourism metadata on both language entries, matching the en map link.
+- Plan note check:
+  - "Six-sided tower (六角樓)": supported. The tower dates from 1936. The market itself dates from 1917.
+  - "Over covered market halls": broadly supported. The tower is the hub where the covered radial aisles and three wings meet. It is a two-storey building at the centre of the halls, not a tower standing on their roofs.
+  - "Beside the Green River": wrong. The market is about 170 m east of the Liuchuan Canal (柳川) waterfront, a different canal. The Green River point is about 0.7 km east-southeast. If water appears next to the market, it should be the Liuchuan, or the note should drop the river.
+- Position: about 0.7 km west-southwest of Taichung Park and about 0.7 km northwest of Miyahara. It is about 0.9 km northwest of Old Taichung Station, 0.42 km north of the Prefectural Hall, 1 km southwest of Yizhong and 2.1 km southeast of the Science Museum.
+- Status: operating. The zh tourism page says hours depend on each stall. No official source found records a heritage designation for the tower.
+
